WEBVTT

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:02.700
COSMOS DARWIN: Hello,

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.340
and thanks for tuning
into Microsoft Build.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:06.600
My name is Cosmos Darwin.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.110
I'm a Product Manager on
the Microsoft Azure team.

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.880
In this session, we're
going to be talking

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:15.300
about shipping physical AI
to the edge using a variety

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.330
of technologies for Microsoft's
Adaptive Cloud approach,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:22.320
including Foundry
Local and Azure Local.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.720
Now, here's specifically
what we're going to cover.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.760
First, I want to take just
a moment to, sort of,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.880
frame the opportunity and the
challenges of physical AI.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.340
We talk a lot about AI,
including this week

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.980
at Microsoft Build, but
often, it's in the context

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.980
of a chatbot on a
computer screen.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:40.470
There's so much more potential
than that when we talk

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.870
about bringing AI out
into the physical world.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.370
That's what we'll frame

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.890
up as the opportunity
for this session.

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:51.210
Then I'll show a demo of
a basic agentic robot

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.610
to help you understand
the opportunity

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.070
for combining language
models and generative AI

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:58.140
with physical systems.

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.630
Then we'll spend most of the
session unpacking how it works.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.820
I want to really give
you a sense for some

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.570
of the core ideas, what's
novel and what isn't relative

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.410
to conventional robotics.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:12.180
Then, finally, I'll just
very briefly mention a few

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.190
of the Adaptive Cloud
technologies that we used

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:17.430
in this demo including small
form factor infrastructure,

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.830
Foundry Local enabled
by Azure Arc,

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:24.210
and the Azure IoT Operations
suite of platform services.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.360
Now, this session is for
builders, so I'm not going

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.160
to give you the marketing pitch
for each of those products,

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.680
but I do just want to give you
a sense for how we used them

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.910
to put together the basic
agentic robot that we showed.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:39.660
Without any further ado
then, let's get started.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.640
What is physical AI?

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.410
Well, when we talk
about AI, we talk a lot

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:46.350
about the transformative
potential, how AI helps

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:49.770
so many people to really
achieve more, to do things

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.990
that they might have thought
before would take a long time.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.270
Suddenly, that time is
compressed and things

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.090
that you would have
considered to be a large,

00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:00.060
challenging task can be
completed very quickly,

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.320
but the benefits from those
advancements have not been

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.850
evenly distributed.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:08.580
An awful lot of the examples
we give are documents

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.790
or spreadsheets, basically,
information workers benefiting

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.040
from the advances of AI.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.310
Let me give you a few
examples of types of work

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.770
that have not really
seen that same change.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.830
For example, performing
inspections or maintenance

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:26.610
or even repairs out in
the physical world.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:30.210
This work has not really
benefited from the advances

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.700
of AI in the last few years.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.270
Similarly, factories, yeah,

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:39.240
you can automate manufacturing
in a factory context.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.670
A lot of things are actually
built outside of a factory.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.950
You can look around and you'll
see job sites everywhere

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:45.780
where construction is happening,

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.850
and you won't see
a lot of robots.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.430
It's people
performing that work.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:54.390
Similarly, in logistics and
distribution and warehousing,

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:58.020
a lot of this looks the
same now as it did a decade

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.850
or even two decades ago.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:04.470
Those gains in productivity have
not really materialized yet.

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:06.930
Those things, once they get
distributed through logistics

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.390
and arrive at
brick-and-mortar retail

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:12.360
or quick-serve restaurants,
a lot of the work

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.030
of stocking shelves or
food prep, similarly,

00:03:15.030 --> 00:03:19.320
has not really benefited from
this advancement with AI

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:21.720
in the last few years.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.660
There's a common theme to
all of these examples,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.510
which is that in each
of these situations,

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:30.210
interacting with other
people and interacting

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.490
with a changing environment
around you, with changes

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.850
in the open world, is a
core part of the work.

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:36.480
This is something

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.240
that historically robots
have struggled with.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.790
Robots excel when you're in
a controlled environment,

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.250
you're doing the exact
same task over and over,

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.740
so a factory is a
great example of this,

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:49.650
but all of those examples
I just gave are domains

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:54.000
where robots have
struggled to make inroads.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.100
As a result, the work
looks an awful lot

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.040
like it has for decades.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.400
However, what's exciting is that
many people, myself included,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.100
believe that that is
beginning to change.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.490
With some of the advances in
recent years with AI models

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:11.970
and being able to run them
locally, it is possible to go

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.990
from robotics to a more
agentic approach to robots

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.640
that may be better able to
do a lot of these tasks.

00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.320
To illustrate that, my team

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:23.780
and I have put together
a basic example.

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:25.490
Now, let me set expectations.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:27.440
This robot I'm about to
show you, it's not going

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.860
to take anyone's job, but
it does serve to sort

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:34.260
of show the potential
of combining AI,

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.800
especially language models,
with physical systems.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.310
Take a look.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:44.610
[ Music ]

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:48.520
Hey, robot, can you
pick up the red cube?

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:56.570
[ Music ]

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.260
That's right and
put it in the bowl.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:01.400
[ Music ]

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:03.860
Wait, I changed my mind.

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.730
Can you give it to me?

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:13.920
[ Music ]

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:14.880
Thank you.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.290
Now pick up the green brick.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:25.620
[ Music ]

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.520
Now put it in this green bowl.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:44.050
[ Music ]

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:44.440
That's right.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:45.830
Well done.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.950
Isn't the robot cute?

00:05:49.950 --> 00:05:53.040
[Laughter] Now look, as
I said, it's a simple

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.070
or a basic example, so that
robot is not about to go

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.140
out into the open
world and address any

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.780
of the scenarios I talked about
at the beginning, but I do want

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:01.800
to reflect on a few

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.640
of the interesting aspects
of what we just saw.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.460
I, as the person interacting
with the robot, did not need

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.730
to adhere to a strict
syntax of commands to sort

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:13.680
of start or stop the robot.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:14.550
I could interact with it

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.340
in a very natural way,
in a very fluid way.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.460
I could just say what
I wanted it to do,

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.800
even if it was a kind
of rambling sentence.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.860
"Hey, robot, please, would
you mind maybe picking

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.990
up that thing there?"

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.980
The robot, because it's an
agentic robot, is able to reason

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:35.730
about what it heard and figure
out what I meant anyway,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:39.150
even though the interaction was
kind of messy, the inputs coming

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.790
from a person were messy.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.160
Likewise, it was able to
respond as the environment

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.280
around it changed, because
it's continuously listening,

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.520
continuously looking, and
when I moved objects on it

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:53.100
or when I changed the
instruction midstream,

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:56.250
it was able to sort of pick
up and adapt and keep going.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:58.320
You can easily imagine
a version of that

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.680
with a different embodiment,
more sophisticated hardware,

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.520
for example, with more different
things that it knows how to do,

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.510
being a helpful collaborator
in spaces where, historically,

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:10.680
people have not been
able to benefit

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.510
from having a helpful
robot collaborator

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.360
because of the
challenges I mentioned.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.390
Okay. How does it actually work?

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.240
Let's unpack a little bit, what
is going on behind the scenes

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.110
in that demo, so that you can
see the pieces that are new

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.390
and novel and then the other
pieces which, actually,

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:30.810
are very much just sort
of conventional robotics.

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:33.870
Everything you saw in the demo,
all of the intelligence required

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.720
to make the robot work as shown,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.290
runs on this small,
industrial computer.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.230
For the demo, we use the
Lenovo ThinkEdge SE100

00:07:43.230 --> 00:07:45.630
because it's sort of
industrial grade.

00:07:45.630 --> 00:07:48.300
It has an optional
NVIDIA discrete GPU,

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.190
but many other devices
would work too.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:52.950
This is just the
one that we chose.

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:54.780
This computer is
physically on the table,

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:55.920
co-located with the robot.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.650
You can see it actually in
the background of the video,

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.500
and it's connected just
over USB, nothing fancy,

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.820
to three peripherals: First,
a UVC-compliant RGB camera,

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:07.860
which is positioned over
the workspace looking

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:09.840
down at the table.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.990
Second, a UAC-compliant
omnidirectional microphone,

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:15.960
which, of course, is used
to capture the speech.

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:20.130
Then finally, the small tabletop
robot arm, which has six degrees

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.020
of freedom and a
seventh if you want

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:24.780
to count the gripper
attachment that it has.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:28.710
By the way, all of these are
deliberately approachable,

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.120
off-the-shelf budget options,

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:31.950
because there's very
little preventing you

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:35.330
from just trying this at
home yourself, as we did.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.130
Now in the video, you can
see each of these things,

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:40.160
but just to sort of show you
clearly, that was the USB camera

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:42.740
over the work surface, and
that's the USB microphone.

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:45.200
These really are -- there's
nothing terribly fancy

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:45.560
about these.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:47.360
It's not a depth camera
or anything like that.

00:08:47.360 --> 00:08:50.610
It's just a classic,
almost like a webcam,

00:08:50.610 --> 00:08:52.140
so that's the hardware setup.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:53.520
Perhaps a little
more interesting is

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:55.020
on the software side.

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.750
On that small form factor
industrial computer,

00:08:57.750 --> 00:09:00.450
we chose to run the Azure
Linux operating system,

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.600
and then Foundry Local, which
you may know as something

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.300
that was originally available
on Windows, then it expanded

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:07.710
to Windows and Android.

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.920
Foundry Local is now newly
packaged as a container image

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.380
that you can run on Linux,
which was a great fit

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.470
for our scenario here.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:19.130
Foundry Local then hosts all
of the AI models that we use

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:21.710
to make the robot
do what it does.

00:09:21.710 --> 00:09:25.250
At runtime, here's how this
actually comes together.

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:28.160
Firstly, audio from the
microphone is split

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.730
into utterances, and then fed
into Nemotron Speech Streaming.

00:09:32.730 --> 00:09:35.520
Nemotron is a
speech-to-text model

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:37.530
that was developed by Nvidia.

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:38.670
It's available for free

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:41.250
in the Foundry Local
catalog, and it's great.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.170
It's one of the best models
in terms of performance

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:45.600
and in terms of very
low error rate.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.210
As you saw in the video,
even though I am facing away

00:09:48.210 --> 00:09:51.570
from the microphone, not
speaking terribly clearly,

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:54.270
there's a bunch of ambient
noise, it was still able to pick

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:57.330
up and transcribe everything
I said very reliably.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:10:01.230
Feeding the audio into Nemotron
Speech gives us back text

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:04.830
of what was said by the user.

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:07.800
In parallel with that,
frames from the camera

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.800
over the work surface are
continuously being captured

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.620
and fed into a variety
of vision models,

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:16.230
including an open
vocabulary detector model,

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:19.860
to recognize everything that
we can see on the surface.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:24.120
That certainly includes colored
blocks and the little bowls

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:26.340
or containers that
we put them in.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.950
It includes a human
hand, for example,

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:29.940
but it's not limited to that.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:31.950
Something we didn't show
in the video is, also,

00:10:31.950 --> 00:10:34.230
just everyday objects
can be recognized

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:37.860
by these detector models,
so sunglasses, for example,

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:40.080
or a wedding ring,
that's no problem.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:41.760
It will pick them up.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.500
Basically, for each
of these detections,

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.510
it's returning the
coordinates within the frame

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:47.970
of where that object was seen.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:51.990
As applicable, it also returns
the orientation, the major axis

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:54.000
of rotation, because, of
course, that's very important

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:57.900
if the robot is going to go
actually pick that object up.

00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.970
The vision pipeline gives
us these coordinates

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:02.040
and angles of rotation.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.510
Now for the most important
part, the reasoning.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:10.290
All of these inputs, the text
from the speech pipeline

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.420
and the coordinates from
the vision pipeline,

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:16.020
are fed into a small language
model that runs locally

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:18.060
on that small
industrial computer.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:21.600
Now for the demo, we chose Quen3
because it's available for free

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:23.910
in the Foundry Local catalog,
and it supports reasoning

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:27.450
and tool calling, but many
other options would likely work

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:28.620
as well.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.870
We chose the 1.7-billion
parameter version,

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:32.820
which most people would say
means it's a small language

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:35.070
model, but if you had
more compute available,

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:36.930
you could also run
a larger version

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:41.220
and quite possibly handle more
complex types of instructions.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:44.490
What's happening here is
given all of the inputs,

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:47.400
the language model is asked
to choose between a set

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:49.560
of tool calls that
are available to it.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:50.610
Now, for our example, again,

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:52.260
it's very basic,
it's very simple.

00:11:52.260 --> 00:11:54.780
We just gave it four tools
that it knows how to call.

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:58.350
Those tools are Pick, so go
pick up an item at a location.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:02.370
Place, put it somewhere at
some other location, or Pick

00:12:02.370 --> 00:12:05.280
and Place, all as one movement.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:06.990
Then the final
tool call is Stop.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.200
When it hears something like in
the video when I say "Wait,"

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:13.080
it interprets that as
a "Stop" tool call.

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:16.110
All the language model does is
decide based on what it sees

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:20.670
and hears, which of these tools
is it being asked to call?

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:22.170
In the example we've
been using, of course,

00:12:22.170 --> 00:12:24.210
it's a pick-and-place
tool call where the

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:27.210
"Pick" target is the red cube;
the "Place" target is the hand,

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:31.020
me, and once it has
done that interpreting,

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:32.460
the rest is pretty
straightforward, right?

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:34.830
That gets converted into
a sequence of movements

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:37.290
that get planned out and then
the robot arm executes them.

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:38.790
This part is really no different

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:40.620
from how it would have
been in the past.

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:44.610
What's so interesting is
the additional flexibility,

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:47.250
the additional almost
humanness that comes

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:51.690
from the robot being able to
interpret very messy inputs

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:54.690
and still figure out what you
meant and then complete the task

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:57.900
if it's among the set
of tools that it has.

00:12:57.900 --> 00:12:58.560
All right.

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.860
There were a number of adaptive
cloud technologies that we used

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:03.900
to make this demo
possible, and so I do want

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.510
to just briefly touch
on each of them.

00:13:06.510 --> 00:13:09.120
The first one is the small
form factor infrastructure,

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.390
that industrial computer that
we use to run the whole demo.

00:13:12.390 --> 00:13:13.560
There are a bunch
of capabilities

00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:15.900
that Microsoft Azure has
offered for several years

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:17.160
that have previously
been limited

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:19.560
to Azure Local on big servers.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:22.140
This is things like provisioning
machines from the cloud

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:24.450
or managing them from the cloud.

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:26.970
What's new is that those
capabilities are now available

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:30.870
in preview for these smaller
industrial form factors,

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:31.950
so take a look.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:36.450
In the Azure portal, I can
go provision a machine.

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:38.820
You'll see when I input
the ownership voucher

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:43.140
for that Lenovo SE100 that
I have, it picks it up,

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:46.050
and I can actually choose what
software stack to install

00:13:46.050 --> 00:13:48.780
onto this computer straight from
the cloud portal without needing

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:50.280
to plug in a
keyboard or monitor.

00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.860
In this case, I chose Azure
Linux, and I could do

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:55.860
that provisioning
entirely from the cloud.

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:59.250
Once I do that, I get a
resource representation

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:02.370
in the Azure Resource
Manager for this computer.

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:04.290
You'll see it looks
and works an awful lot

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:05.850
like a cloud virtual machine.

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:08.220
It's a provisioned
machine resource type,

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:11.400
and it has a bunch of things
that I get visibility into

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:14.520
and management functions
that I can perform.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:17.820
One simple example is I can
grant access to this machine

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:19.320
to a colleague of mine directly

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:22.590
from the cloud portal
using Microsoft Entra ID,

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:25.620
which means I don't need to have
a password on a Post-it note

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:29.370
that I've written down or
SSH key that I send to them.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:33.240
This is a much more robust way
of managing a device like this,

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:35.130
which is going to be
really important if I want

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:39.120
to take this system and
have hundreds or thousands

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.370
of them distributed
across many locations.

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:42.240
Being able to manage it all

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:43.410
through the cloud
control plane is going

00:14:43.410 --> 00:14:45.660
to be extremely helpful.

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.420
That's one of the things
that we did in this demo.

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:50.970
Another one which I've already
mentioned is we used Foundry

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.670
Local, and specifically,
the flavor of Foundry Local

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:58.950
that is newly available now as
a container image for Linux.

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:01.740
On our small form factor
industrial computer,

00:15:01.740 --> 00:15:03.480
we installed Azure
Linux as I said.

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:04.860
Now also to keep all
of this straight,

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:09.660
we also just installed K3s, like
a small, single-node Kubernetes,

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:13.200
and then we packaged our
application code as a container.

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:14.700
That code is just Python.

00:15:14.700 --> 00:15:18.360
It's really not terribly fancy.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.120
Alongside that container, we
then deployed Foundry Local

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:24.210
in a container, and Foundry
Local exposes an open AI

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:26.130
compatible REST API
that we can use

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:28.950
to make inferencing
requests, and this is great

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:31.380
because it means we don't
need to host the AI models

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.810
in our own code, which
can be very clunky.

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:36.930
It can kind of -- you can run
into challenges related to size

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.840
and agility and all
sorts of reasons

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:42.780
that you don't really want
to do it that way, but also,

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:44.910
there are a few specific
advantages to this approach

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:46.950
that I thought were
pretty compelling.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:50.580
The first is Foundry Local gives
you a trusted, authoritative way

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:54.750
to access a safe
instance of a model.

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.280
In our demo, we wanted Quen3,

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:58.380
the 1.7-billion
parameter version.

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:03.420
All we had to do was give its
name in our configuration file

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:07.620
and then Foundry Local goes out
and retrieves a trusted copy

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:10.260
of that model from the
Foundry Local online catalog,

00:16:10.260 --> 00:16:13.050
downloads it to run entirely
locally, and from that point on,

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:14.850
there are no calls
to the cloud at all.

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:18.270
It's just running inside of that
Foundry Local container for you.

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:20.220
You can see in the catalog here,

00:16:20.220 --> 00:16:21.570
there are so many
options available,

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:23.640
including Nemotron
Speech, the other model

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.250
that we used in the demo.

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:27.840
Once the model is running,

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:31.260
you can then just access it
using an endpoint very much the

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:33.270
way you would if it was
running in the cloud.

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:34.740
It's like a drop-in replacement

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:37.380
for making an API call
to a public cloud.

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:41.130
Instead of accessing, say,
Azure to run the model,

00:16:41.130 --> 00:16:42.870
the model is running locally

00:16:42.870 --> 00:16:45.420
on the small form factor device
right next to your code.

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:46.500
What's great about
that, of course,

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.080
is the latency is super
predictable, and for a system

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.540
that is going to be out in the
physical world interacting

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:53.700
with people, it all needs
to be as close to real time

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:56.970
as you can get, so
this is a great fit.

00:16:56.970 --> 00:16:58.680
Now speaking of real
time, the other thing

00:16:58.680 --> 00:16:59.820
that Foundry Local takes care

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:04.710
of that I loved is it handles
detecting the available hardware

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:09.090
accelerators in your hardware
and lighting them up for you.

00:17:09.090 --> 00:17:12.900
In our case, our Lenovo
SE100 has an Nvidia GPU.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:17.490
It has an RTX 2000 in it, and
when you're using Foundry Local

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:20.460
on top of the small form
factor infrastructure managed

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:23.310
through Azure, the whole
top-to-bottom stack can be lined

00:17:23.310 --> 00:17:24.180
up for you.

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:27.120
Everything from the kernel mode
driver to the device plug-in,

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:29.430
to the user mode
libraries that are inside

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:32.430
of the Foundry Local container,
all of that is lined up.

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:34.440
Actually, as you
see in the diagram,

00:17:34.440 --> 00:17:37.200
your own application code
doesn't even need to do anything

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:38.370
with the GPU, actually.

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:41.010
You're still benefiting from
GPU-accelerated inference,

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:43.020
because as you make
those inference requests

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:45.510
to Foundry Local, they
happen much quicker

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:46.740
because the GPU is there.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:50.040
In this instance, it sped
up reasoning by Quen

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:51.540
by a factor of many times.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:52.560
We tried it both ways.

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:55.170
This was a huge boost.

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:56.880
Here you can actually
see a screenshot.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:17:58.260
This is a pre-release tool,

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:00.810
so it may change a little
before we release it,

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:05.280
but you can see a screenshot of
that Nvidia RTX 2000E running

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:07.440
in our device, and
in the Azure portal,

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.380
I can see the usage of that GPU.

00:18:10.380 --> 00:18:13.770
Every time Quen is asked to
interpret some instructions,

00:18:13.770 --> 00:18:16.290
you can see a small
spike in the GPU usage,

00:18:16.290 --> 00:18:18.870
which shows you that
it's being used.

00:18:18.870 --> 00:18:19.110
All right.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:20.970
The last piece of the
puzzle I want to talk

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:24.300
about is Azure IoT Operations.

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:27.180
IoT Operations is a suite
of platform services

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:30.030
that enables you to connect
to devices and protocols

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:31.860
in your local environment.

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:34.860
Then connect the data from
them up into the cloud,

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:38.130
and potentially, route commands
back, such as mission plans

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:41.030
for a robot, for example.

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.520
IoT Operations includes a
number of built-in connectors

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:47.810
that are really handy for things
like OPC UA, HTTP, ONVIF,

00:18:47.810 --> 00:18:50.780
and it also includes an
industrial grade MQTT broker

00:18:50.780 --> 00:18:54.890
that you can configure so that
as data flows into the broker,

00:18:54.890 --> 00:18:57.680
you can then configure how
it gets transformed and sent

00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:58.940
up to the cloud, for example,

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:02.250
for you to do analysis
in Microsoft Fabric.

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:04.590
Now in our example,
there are some things

00:19:04.590 --> 00:19:06.600
that we were interested
in monitoring.

00:19:06.600 --> 00:19:10.290
One of them is the robot's
span or reach, right?

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:13.530
Our robot, of course, is very
small, and it can only reach,

00:19:13.530 --> 00:19:15.780
I don't know, maybe half
a meter or something.

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.910
The field of view of the camera
is actually larger than that,

00:19:17.910 --> 00:19:19.920
and so occasionally,
you have the situation

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:22.650
where the camera can see an
object, and the robot believes

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:25.110
that it's being asked to go
pick or place that object,

00:19:25.110 --> 00:19:29.280
but it actually can't reach it,
as you see in this image here.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:32.420
Now, in the case of just a
single robot on a tabletop,

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:34.730
of course, it's not that hard
to diagnose this yourself,

00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:36.620
but you can imagine
deploying this

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:38.660
across many locations
in the field.

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:41.600
Being able to get real-time
telemetry and intelligence off

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:43.910
of a system like
this is everything.

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:48.060
That is where Azure IoT
Operations is so valuable.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:48.960
Let me show you.

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:51.450
On the K3 instance that we run

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:54.060
on this small
industrial computer,

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:56.820
we deployed Azure IoT Operations
using the extension approach.

00:19:56.820 --> 00:20:00.000
Both IoT Operations and Foundry
are available as extensions,

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.660
which means it's just a couple
of clicks or a single YAML file,

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:05.330
and you're off to the races.

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:07.520
Once IoT Operations
is installed,

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:10.490
you get this really approachable
operations experience,

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:13.520
which would be usable not
only by a more IT persona,

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:15.080
but actually an
operations persona

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:16.610
who maybe has specific knowledge

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:20.150
about millimeter offsets
and things like that.

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:22.910
You can configure
how data should flow

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:26.820
from a discovered asset in your
environment, in this case,

00:20:26.820 --> 00:20:30.210
the robot arm, up into a
workspace in the cloud,

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:33.000
and you can even
transform that data here.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:36.420
For example, if you needed to
do a unit conversion or add

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:39.870
or remove additional properties,
like in our example,

00:20:39.870 --> 00:20:42.780
we're just trying to see if the
coordinates are safe to reach.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:44.580
For example, we could
remove the z-coordinate,

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:46.200
because it doesn't
really matter here.

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:49.170
You can do that through this
very approachable interface,

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:51.810
so that when the data
arrives in Microsoft Fabric,

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:54.600
it's ready for your data
team to just interact with.

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:55.590
It's already cleaned up.

00:20:55.590 --> 00:20:58.890
It's already formatted in a
way that they'll understand.

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:03.930
For us, we configured Azure
IoT Operations to stream all

00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:05.460
of the coordinate
requests from the robot.

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:08.280
Every time that it is
asked to go grab something

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:09.960
at a particular location,

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:12.840
it streams back what coordinates
it was asked to grab from,

00:21:12.840 --> 00:21:16.050
and we can then analyze if
those were safe or unsafe.

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:18.750
All of that is coming in
real time off of the system,

00:21:18.750 --> 00:21:21.720
so you can imagine if you were
going to deploy this broadly,

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:25.110
this would be an extremely
valuable capability.

00:21:25.110 --> 00:21:27.810
Okay. Where does that leave us?

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:29.580
Advances in AI together

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:32.460
with Azure's Adaptive Cloud
approach are making it easier

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:36.840
than ever to build and operate
intelligent physical systems.

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:39.420
This year and I think in the
years ahead, we are going

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.600
to have to update our
notion of what types

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:45.480
of tasks it is
possible to extend AI

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:49.470
to with an agentic
approach to robotics.

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:51.750
If you're as excited about
this as I am, I encourage you

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:53.460
to get started right now.

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:56.130
All of the things that we showed
here are available off the shelf

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:57.300
for you to try.

00:21:57.300 --> 00:21:59.250
The small form factor
infrastructure is available

00:21:59.250 --> 00:22:00.480
in preview now.

00:22:00.480 --> 00:22:02.580
You can take it for a spin.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:05.190
Foundry Local as a
container image for Linux,

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:07.230
enabled by Azure Arc,
is also available

00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:08.280
as a preview right now.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.710
You can go try that out
for a scenario like ours

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:13.140
or many other scenarios as well.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:14.610
Of course, last but not least,

00:22:14.610 --> 00:22:18.540
Azure IoT Operations is also
available for you to try.

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:19.680
All right.

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:20.820
That's it for me.

00:22:20.820 --> 00:22:24.310
Thank you so much for watching
and enjoy the rest of Build.
